  "answer_text": "**Absolutely \u2014 what we eat matters enormously!** Here's a comprehensive breakdown of why:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\udd57 Diet and Overall Health\n\nDiet plays a critical role in shaping the health and well-being of both individuals and populations, and unhealthy diets are a major risk factor for disease and disability. As the American Medical Association puts it, what you eat plays a leading role in your health and well-being, and eating healthy helps protect against many chronic diseases such as heart disease, prediabetes, type 2 diabetes, and obesity.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\uddec Food Interacts With Your Body at a Deep Level\n\nThe intake of food is more than just a necessary process for ensuring the functionality of the human body. Nutritional components become a part of us, interacting with our gut microbiota, immune system, and metabolism.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\udda0 Diet and Disease Risk\n\nFor decades, it has been known that the occurrence and the course of a variety of typical Western diseases \u2014 such as cancer, stroke, and myocardial infarction \u2014 are affected by diet.\n\n- Recent research shows that a diet rich in red meat and processed meat leads to a high risk of cancer, especially colorectal cancer.\n- Substantial evidence indicates that eating more vegetables and fruits and less meat is able to decrease overall cancer risk and improve health.\n- Healthy diets help to protect against noncommunicable diseases (NCDs), including diabetes, heart disease, stroke, and cancer.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\udde0 Diet and Mental Health\n\nEating a poor diet reduces physical and mental health because eating healthy allows people to be more active. Two-thirds of people who eat fresh fruit and vegetables daily report no mental health issues, compared to those who have some level of mental health problems and have generally reported eating less healthy foods and more unhealthy foods.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcb0 The Economic Cost of Poor Diet\n\nAccording to the U.S. Department of Agriculture, a healthier diet could prevent at least $71 billion per year in medical costs and lost lives \u2014 and that number may be underestimated because it only accounts for diet-related coronary heart disease, stroke, cancer, and diabetes.\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 What a Healthy Diet Looks Like\n\nHealthy diets come in many forms, but the four core principles of **adequacy, balance, moderation, and diversity** should be the foundation of any healthy diet. A variety of minimally processed and unprocessed foods low in unhealthy fats, free sugars, and sodium are the foundation of any healthy diet.\n\nThe exact make-up of a balanced and healthy diet will vary depending on individual characteristics such as age, gender, lifestyle, and degree of physical activity, as well as cultural context, locally available foods, and dietary customs.\n\n---\n\nIn summary, **yes \u2014 what we eat profoundly matters**, affecting everything from our risk of chronic disease to our mental well-being and even our economic burden on society. Small, consistent dietary choices can have a huge long-term impact on quality of life.",
